.TH TELEGRAM-NOTIFIERD 8 "2026-09-15" "telegram-notifier 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
telegram\-notifierd \- daemon that delivers queued notifications to Telegram
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B telegram\-notifierd
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR LEVEL ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B telegram\-notifierd
listens on a Unix domain socket and forwards the messages it receives to the
Telegram Bot API. It is normally started by systemd as the
.B telegram\-notifier
service and is not meant to be run by hand, except with
.B \-\-check
to validate a configuration change.
.PP
Every accepted message is written to the on-disk queue under
.I /var/lib/telegram\-notifier/queue
.I before
the client is told the message was queued, so an acknowledged message
survives a crash, a restart or a reboot. Entries are written to a temporary
file and renamed into place, so a partially written entry is never visible.
.PP
Delivery is attempted by a single worker, highest priority first and oldest
first within a priority. Failures that a retry could fix (network errors,
Telegram 5xx replies, rate limiting) are retried with exponential backoff; a
.B 429
reply is honoured for exactly the delay Telegram asks for. Failures a retry
cannot fix (an invalid token, an unknown chat, malformed markup) are not
retried. When a message is abandoned, either because it exhausted
.B max_attempts
or because the failure was permanent, it is moved to
.I /var/lib/telegram\-notifier/failed
with its last error recorded, rather than being discarded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Configuration file to read. Default
.IR /etc/telegram\-notifier/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-check
Parse and validate the configuration, print a summary and exit without
binding the socket. Run this before restarting the service after an edit.
It checks that a bot token can be read, but does not contact Telegram, so a
syntactically valid yet wrong token still passes; verify one with
.I https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getMe
instead.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-level " " \fILEVEL\fR
Override
.B [service] log_level
from the configuration file. The
.B RUST_LOG
environment variable, if set, takes precedence over both.
.SH ACCESS CONTROL
The control socket is created owned by the daemon's user and the group named
in
.BR "[service] socket_group" ,
with mode
.BR socket_mode .
With the shipped defaults that is
.BR telegram\-notify ,
mode
.BR 0660 :
membership of that group is exactly what grants the right to send
notifications. There is no other authentication, so treat group membership as
the security boundary.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop accepting new messages, finish the request in flight and exit. Anything
still queued stays on disk and is retried on the next start.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/telegram-notifier/config.toml
Main configuration file. See
.BR telegram\-notifier.toml (5).
.TP
.I /etc/telegram-notifier/bot-token
Default location of the bot token, referenced by
.BR bot_token_file .
.TP
.I /run/telegram-notifier/notifier.sock
Control socket.
.TP
.I /var/lib/telegram-notifier/queue
Pending messages, one JSON file each.
.TP
.I /var/lib/telegram-notifier/failed
Abandoned messages, kept for inspection. Nothing removes these
automatically.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Logs go to standard error, which systemd records in the journal:
.PP
.RS
.EX
journalctl \-u telegram\-notifier \-f
.EE
.RE
.PP
Queue depth and delivery counters are available through the client:
.PP
.RS
.EX
telegram\-notify \-\-status
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR telegram\-notify (1),
.BR telegram\-notifier.toml (5),
.BR systemctl (1)
